---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import BeginnerNote from '../components/BeginnerNote.astro';
---

<MainLayout title="Uses Beyond Code" subtitle="Claude Code isn't just for developers" module={10}>

  <!-- 1. Intro -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">You're probably sleeping on these</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Most people think Claude Code is just for writing software. It's not. It's a general-purpose tool that can read, write, analyze, transform, and create almost anything that lives on your computer. Here are the use cases you're probably sleeping on.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Everything in this module works the same way you've been using Claude Code in previous modules — you type a message, Claude does the work. The only difference is the <em>kind</em> of work you're asking for.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. CAD &amp; Technical Drawing -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">CAD &amp; Technical Drawing</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Claude can read and modify SVG files, generate parametric designs, and even work with DXF formats. If your drawing is vector-based, Claude can understand and edit it.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Read floor-plan.svg and add a 3m x 4m room to the east wall with a door opening facing north"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the SVG, understands the spatial structure, adds the new room geometry with correct dimensions, places the door opening, and saves the updated file.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      SVG files are images made of mathematical shapes instead of pixels. Because they're described in code, Claude can read and edit them like any other text file — adding walls, moving elements, changing dimensions.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Presentations &amp; Slide Decks -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Presentations &amp; Slide Decks</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Generate HTML-based presentations or modify existing slide content. Better than fighting PowerPoint — and the results are shareable as a single file.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Create a 10-slide presentation about our Q2 results. Use the data from quarterly-report.csv. Make it clean with our brand colors."
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the CSV to extract the data, generates a complete slide deck (HTML or Markdown-based), applies styling and brand colors, and outputs a presentation you can open in any browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. CMS Data Wrangling &amp; Cleanup -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">CMS Data Wrangling &amp; Cleanup</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Clean up messy CMS exports, deduplicate entries, standardize formatting, and prepare data for import into a new system. No more manual spreadsheet surgery.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Read cms-export.csv — standardize all phone numbers to +61 format, remove duplicate entries based on email, and flag any rows with missing required fields"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the CSV, applies phone number formatting rules, identifies and removes duplicates by email, flags incomplete rows, and outputs a clean version ready for import.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      This is the kind of task that takes hours in Excel. Claude does it in seconds and doesn't miss any rows. It's especially useful after migrating between CMS platforms.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. CSV &amp; Data Pipeline Work -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">CSV &amp; Data Pipeline Work</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Parse, transform, merge, and analyze data files without opening Excel or writing Python scripts. Just describe what you want and Claude handles the rest.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Merge sales-2024.csv and sales-2025.csv, add a 'year' column, remove rows where revenue is 0, and sort by date descending"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads both files, merges them into a single dataset, adds the year column, filters out zero-revenue rows, sorts by date, and saves the result as a new CSV.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. PDF Analysis -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">PDF Analysis</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Read contracts, extract key terms from invoices, summarize long documents. Claude can process PDFs and pull out exactly the information you need.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Read contract.pdf and extract: parties involved, contract value, start date, end date, termination clause, and any penalty clauses"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the PDF, identifies the relevant sections, extracts the structured data you asked for, and presents it in a clear summary — no more reading 40-page contracts line by line.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      This works with any PDF — contracts, invoices, reports, manuals. If you can read it, Claude can read it and pull out the parts you care about.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Browser Automation -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Browser Automation</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      With the Chrome MCP tools, Claude can navigate websites, fill forms, scrape data, and test user flows — all from your terminal.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Go to our staging site, fill out the contact form with test data, submit it, and verify the success message appears"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> opens Chrome, navigates to the URL, identifies form fields, fills them with test data, clicks the submit button, waits for the response, and confirms whether the success message appeared.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      This is like having someone sit at your computer and click through a website for you — except they never get bored and they can do it a hundred times in a row.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Image &amp; Screenshot Analysis -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Image &amp; Screenshot Analysis</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Show Claude a screenshot and ask what's wrong, what to improve, or how to replicate it. Claude can see images and reason about their content.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Look at screenshot.png — the layout is broken on mobile. What CSS is causing the overflow and how do I fix it?"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> analyzes the image, identifies the visual issue (e.g., an element overflowing the viewport), traces it to likely CSS causes, and suggests specific fixes with code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. File Batch Operations -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">File Batch Operations</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Rename hundreds of files, reorganize folder structures, convert between formats. Tasks that would take an hour of clicking take Claude seconds.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Rename all photos in the events/ folder from IMG_XXXX.jpg to 2024-event-001.jpg format, numbered sequentially by date modified"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the directory listing, sorts files by modification date, renames each file with the new naming convention, and confirms the results.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Think about every time you've manually renamed files one by one. Now imagine describing the pattern you want and having it done instantly. That's this.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. Writing &amp; Content -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Writing &amp; Content</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Draft blog posts, compliance documents, email templates — in your voice and style. Claude can read your existing content and match the tone.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Write a blog post about hail damage prevention for homeowners. Match the tone of our existing blog at content/blog/. Keep it under 800 words with practical tips."
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads your existing blog posts to learn the voice and style, writes a new post matching that tone, stays within the word count, and saves it in the right format for your CMS.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 11. Spreadsheet Transformations -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Spreadsheet Transformations</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Transform Excel and CSV data without opening Excel. Pivot, filter, aggregate — describe the output you want and Claude builds it.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Read timesheet.xlsx — create a summary showing total hours per employee per week, and flag anyone over 40 hours"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> reads the spreadsheet, groups data by employee and week, calculates total hours, flags any entries over 40 hours, and outputs a clean summary table.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 12. API Debugging &amp; Testing -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">API Debugging &amp; Testing</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Hit API endpoints, inspect responses, test webhooks — without Postman. Claude can construct requests, send them, and verify the results end-to-end.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="Test the POST endpoint at /api/bookings with sample booking data, show me the response, and check if the record appears in Supabase"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> constructs a properly formatted request with sample data, sends it to the endpoint, shows the response status and body, then queries Supabase to verify the record was created.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      An API endpoint is just a URL that your app listens on. When you "test" it, you're sending it fake data to make sure it processes it correctly. Claude handles all the technical details of making the request.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 13. Automation &amp; Scheduling -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Automation &amp; Scheduling</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Claude can work while you sleep. Set up recurring tasks that run automatically on a schedule, or create non-interactive scripts that process data without you watching.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command="/loop 5m check the deploy status and alert if it fails"
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> runs the deploy status check every 5 minutes, compares it to baseline state, and alerts you only if something fails. You don't have to babysit.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={'claude schedule "every day at 9am, check Sentry for new errors and create tickets"'}
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> cloud-hosted agent that wakes up at 9am, queries Sentry's API for errors from the past day, extracts the signal, and creates tickets for the top issues. Fully automated.
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={'claude -p "analyze error.log and suggest fixes" < error.log'}
    />

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      <strong>What Claude does:</strong> runs non-interactively (no chat interface), reads the error log from stdin, analyzes it once, outputs suggestions, and exits. Perfect for cron jobs or CI/CD pipelines.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Claude can work while you sleep. Schedule tasks to run automatically, check status on cron, create tickets from monitoring systems — set it and forget it.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 14. Real-World Productivity Numbers -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-purple/20 bg-purple-50 p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-purple">Real-World Productivity Numbers</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-secondary">
      These aren't marketing claims — they're from Stack Overflow surveys, GitHub data, and enterprise adoption studies.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-3">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-text">4 hours per week saved per developer</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">That's $320/week at $80/hr, or $16,640/year per person.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-text">2–10x development velocity after adoption</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Wide range reflects team experience, task type, and prompt quality. Most teams see 3–5x within 2 weeks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-text">80% faster individual task execution</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Average across debugging, refactoring, testing, and documentation tasks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-text">30% fewer iterations needed</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Claude-assisted code requires less review cycles; fewer back-and-forths.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-text">4% of public GitHub commits are Claude-authored</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">135,000 commits per day as of February 2026. Still growing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 15. Summary card -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-purple/20 bg-purple-50 p-6 md:p-8">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-purple-200 bg-surface p-5">
      <p class="text-lg font-bold text-purple">The bottom line</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-text-secondary">
        Claude Code reads files, writes files, runs commands, and browses the web. If a task involves any combination of those four things, Claude can probably help — whether it's code or not.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
          <span><strong>Read</strong> — PDFs, CSVs, images, spreadsheets, any file</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
          <span><strong>Write</strong> — documents, presentations, data files, code</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
          <span><strong>Run</strong> — scripts, batch operations, API calls, tests</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
          <span><strong>Browse</strong> — navigate sites, fill forms, scrape data</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      You've completed Claude Code University. Go build something.
    </p>
  </section>

</MainLayout>
